{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790258774,"nonce":"zbno2k0r4fjV9BysZq6Biq3NGVE=","title":"Python: a loop over an aliased range builds the list, and a direct counted loop rebinds its target on an empty range","message":"Every speed-center kernel binds `xrange = range` under a version check. The typer knows the name as the builtin, but `for_with_body` counts only under the literal name `range`, so `for i in xrange(n)` built the range with zb_list_range (a fill loop, a pending check, frees) and walked it with a bounds-checked load: a 40 MB list per MonteCarlo call, two million small lists per SparseMatMult iteration, one per bit in FFT; hexiom2, chaos, pyaes, nqueens, richards, float and meteor pay it too.\n\nSeparately, the counted-loop arm that counts in the target itself assigned the target the range's start even when the range was empty; Python leaves a bound target untouched on a zero-trip loop:\n\n    def f(n):\n        i = 7\n        for i in range(n):\n            pass\n        return i\n    print(f(0))   # CPython 7\n\nFix: aliases.rs rewrites reads of a builtin bound under another name (and not rebound) to the builtin before inference, so every range site counts; the direct arm runs its loop and end fixup under `if ran`. A `range` call with arguments the builtin does not take is lowered through its value, so it raises TypeError at run time as the aliased call did.","files":null}]}